b/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186541589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542100 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186541661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542100 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186541762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542100 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186541875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542100 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542100 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542100 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186541576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542100 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f7b7ca88-f1ff-5236-b465-788cafd4842d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542191 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542191 #edb/ref 1069 "q-170401" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542191 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542191 #edb/ref 1071 "The maximum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=-4{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $5.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542191 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "afcfe65d-5017-5ca6-9a0c-f6939bdd2a4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542191 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542227 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542227 #edb/ref 1069 "q-138129" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542227 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542227 #edb/ref 1071 "The maximum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=-3{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $5.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542227 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f0a5dfca-c787-55e8-9cb2-9d81fe185f23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542227 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
